PART III — THE SCIENTIFIC FOUNDATION

3.1 Formal Model

Let H(t) represent the full human cognitive-behavioral state at time t. This includes:

· Physical routines (sleep, meals, exercise, medication)
· Social patterns (calls, messages, visits)
· Financial behaviors (bills, spending, savings)
· Environmental activity (location, movement, home interactions)
· Emotional indicators (voice tone, typing rhythm, inactivity duration)

CGA continuously learns the conditional probability:

P(H_{t+1} | H_t, H_{t-1}, ..., H_{t-n})

This is the expected next state given history.

Cold-start solution (v2.0): For new users, the model begins with a population prior — a pre-trained distribution segmented by age, chronotype, and basic self-report. As personal data accumulate, the system shifts via Bayesian updating toward an entirely individual model. The population prior decays smoothly, preventing the system from being blind during the critical early adoption period.

When the actual observed state diverges significantly:

D_KL(Observed || Expected) > threshold

...the system triggers a lightweight anomaly classifier. This is not merely a detection flag; it maps the deviation into one of five categories (see Layer 3), assigning confidence and severity before any intervention decision is made. The classifier ensures that Tier 3 and Tier 4 actions are never fired on raw statistical noise.

This is personalized behavioral modeling at the individual level — a technique now validated by peer-reviewed science.

Research published in 2025 demonstrates the viability of training recurrent neural networks for behavior modeling with next-day prediction, using a normal distribution over prediction errors to identify significant deviations while accounting for temporal patterns. The best models achieve a mean squared error in next-day routine prediction of 4.38% — precise enough for real-world deployment. nih

3.2 The Digital Phenotype

Each user develops what researchers now call a digital phenotype: a unique, longitudinal behavioral signature derived from passive data.

Digital phenotyping enables access to objective, real-time data streams that reflect an individual's behavioral patterns across daily environments and time periods. It produces a richer, multimodal representation of fluctuations and allows detection of subtle changes that might precede clinical deterioration or significant life events. GlobalRPH

CGA's core engine builds this phenotype for every user and maintains it continuously. Crucially, the user can inspect and correct any inferred pattern, making the phenotype transparent rather than opaque.

3.3 Cognitive Digital Twins

The long-term scientific vision goes further:

Multimodal foundation models are reshaping digital health by enabling unified representations across text, speech, vision, and time-series data derived from wearables — creating the groundwork for cognitive digital twins: dynamic, personalized simulations of human cognition. ScienceDirect

