PART IV — ARCHITECTURE

Layer 1: Data Ingestion (The Sensing Layer)

CGA collects from:

· Smartphone: usage patterns, screen time, app activity, call/message frequency, location, accelerometer, GPS movement
· Wearables: heart rate, sleep cycles, HRV, step count, body temperature
· Smart home sensors (optional, non-invasive): door open/close, appliance use, motion detection
· Connected apps: calendar, banking, email metadata (not content), medication trackers, health records

An IoT framework using non-wearable sensors enables real-time monitoring and anomaly detection while processing data locally, minimizing privacy concerns and ensuring immediate data availability — allowing detection of unusual behavioral patterns early without requiring intrusive hardware. nih

Key design principle: no camera, no microphone content, no message reading. CGA monitors patterns, not content.

User identity separation (v2.0): The sensing layer includes logic to distinguish the primary user from other household members who may interact with shared devices, preventing phenotype contamination.

Layer 2: Behavioral Modeling (The Learning Layer)

The engine evolves with deployment scale:

· Phase 1 (MVP): A simple, interpretable model (e.g., Gaussian process or Poisson process of daily event times) that runs entirely on-device with minimal compute. It learns the user's typical medication-taking window and routine timing, proving detection quality without deep learning complexity.
· Phase 2+: Recurrent Neural Networks (LSTM/GRU) for temporal pattern learning, transformer-based sequence models for long-range dependency detection, and unsupervised clustering to identify and label routine patterns.
· Continuous: Bayesian updating to continuously refine the personal model as life changes.
· Privacy-preserving: Federated learning so model training happens on-device — raw behavioral data never leaves the phone. Only anonymized, aggregate model weight updates are shared for global improvement.

Consistently tracking activities of daily living can provide valuable insights into whether individuals can complete expected routines — and systematic deviations from established patterns are among the most reliable early indicators of cognitive and physical change. Sage Journals

Layer 3: Anomaly Detection (The Guardian Layer)

CGA classifies deviations into five operational categories:

1. Forgotten Tasks — something expected didn't happen (medication not taken, call not made, bill not paid)
2. Behavioral Drift — gradual change in pattern that may signal health or emotional state shift
3. Risk Events — unusual behavior with potential negative consequence (abnormal financial transaction, unusual location, extreme inactivity)
4. Opportunity Windows — positive deviations (unusually good sleep, free time, high-energy period) that can be leveraged
5. Emergency Signals — critical deviation requiring immediate escalation (no movement for extended period, irregular physiological signals)

A lightweight anomaly classifier, triggered by significant KL-divergence, assigns each anomaly to one of these categories with a confidence score. This step prevents misclassification of benign novelty as a threat.

Layer 4: Intervention (The Communication Layer)

CGA communicates through:

· Mobile notifications (gentle, non-intrusive, contextually timed)
· Voice check-ins (optional morning/evening conversational summaries)
· Family/caregiver dashboard (opt-in, designed to provide reassurance — “Mom’s routine is normal today” — not just alerts, to reduce caregiver burnout)
· Emergency escalation (with explicit user consent — alert contacts if critical thresholds are crossed)

Interventions follow a tiered response protocol:

Tier 1 → Soft reminder ("You haven't called your daughter this week.")
Tier 2 → Contextual alert ("You missed your medication this morning and your heart rate is elevated.")
Tier 3 → Proactive action ("Your electricity bill is due tomorrow. Would you like me to schedule payment?")
Tier 4 → Emergency alert (contact caregiver or emergency services with full consent pre-authorization)

Trust budget mechanism (v2.0): The system maintains a dynamic trust budget per user. If the user dismisses an excessive number of alerts as irrelevant, the system automatically tightens detection thresholds or reduces intrusion frequency until trust recovers. This prevents the “cry wolf” effect and ensures long-term adherence.

