PART VI — ETHICS AND PRIVACY FRAMEWORK

This is not optional. It is the foundation.

The fast advancement of AI health monitoring technologies has raised critical ethical concerns, particularly around data privacy, algorithmic bias, informed consent, and the opacity of automated decision-making — challenges that must be addressed by design, not as afterthought. Frontiers

CGA's ethical framework rests on six pillars:

1. Radical Transparency with Inference Disclosure
The user always knows exactly what CGA is monitoring and why. Every alert includes not just the raw data, but the inferred pattern: “You usually call your daughter on Tuesdays; this week you haven’t” rather than “Call log anomaly #472.” Users can inspect and, if needed, correct these inferences, giving them agency over the model’s understanding of their life.

2. On-Device Processing
Behavioral modeling occurs locally. No raw behavioral data is transmitted to servers. Only anonymized, aggregate model weights are used for improvement.

3. Layered Consent
Users enable each data source individually. They can revoke access at any time. Family dashboards and emergency escalation require explicit, separate opt-in.

4. Human Override
CGA never acts autonomously. It informs. It suggests. The human decides. Always. The system is explicitly designed so that the user can disagree with or ignore any alert — and the model learns from that correction without penalizing the user.

5. Anti-Dependency Design
CGA is calibrated to preserve cognitive autonomy, not replace it. The challenge lies in balancing AI's efficiency with the preservation of human cognitive engagement — users must remain active participants, verifying outputs, questioning assumptions, and applying their own judgment. Without this balance, AI may inadvertently foster complacency and erode the very vigilance it is meant to protect. Scholarpublishing

6. Dignified Offboarding
Users can delete their digital phenotype entirely at any time. CGA’s federated learning design ensures that a departing user’s model contributions are efficiently erased from the global model without requiring full retraining, respecting the right to be forgotten under GDPR and equivalent regulations.

