PART VII — FAILURE AND BOUNDARY CONDITIONS

A guardian system must be designed from the boundary inward. These are the hard, pre-answered questions:

What happens when the user disagrees with the guardian?
If CGA says “You missed your medication” and the user says “I took it,” the system accepts the user’s explicit correction. That event is recorded not as a failure, but as a model refinement point. The system learns, “At this time, under these conditions, my sensor pattern was incomplete.” Trust is built through graceful contradiction.

Can the user lie to the guardian?
Yes — intentionally off-pattern behavior (a surprise, a secret trip, a chosen deviation) is not “failure.” CGA distinguishes between unintended drift and volitional divergence by learning to recognize deliberately irregular patterns when they are later confirmed as intentional, or when the user tags a period as “vacation,” “privacy mode,” etc.

How are shared devices and environments handled?
The ingestion layer includes user-disambiguation logic to prevent phenotype contamination from spouses, visitors, or children using the same tablet or triggering the same door sensor. This is essential for household deployment.

What is the false-positive failure mode?
Even a 1% daily false-positive rate yields 3.65 false alarms per user per year. In a guardian system, false alarms erode trust exponentially. The trust budget mechanism (Layer 4) actively monitors user dismissal behavior and adjusts sensitivity to maintain long-term credibility.

Regulatory boundary:
While CGA is not a medical device, its proximity to medication adherence and emergency escalation demands proactive regulatory scoping. From Phase 0, we engage medical device regulatory experts to ensure compliance and to determine whether any feature set falls under FDA, MHRA, or EU MDR oversight.

