A.1 Proof of Lemma 2.1 (Personal Model Convergence)

Statement restated from Section 2.1.
Proof. We consider the regularized stochastic optimization problem
\min_{\theta} F(\theta) = -\mathbb{E}_{H\sim\mathcal{D}}[\log P(H_{1:t} \mid \theta)] + \frac{\lambda}{2}\|\theta - \hat\theta_0\|_2^2.


The on‑device update uses a stochastic proximal gradient step:
\hat\theta_{t+1} = \hat\theta_t + \eta_t\bigl[\nabla_\theta \ell_t(\hat\theta_t) - \lambda(\hat\theta_t - \hat\theta_0)\bigr],


with $\ell_t(\theta) = \log P(H_{t+1}\mid H_{1:t},\theta)$.

Because the objective $F$ is $\lambda$‑strongly convex (the proximal term adds a quadratic), the standard convergence theorem for stochastic gradient descent on strongly convex functions (e.g., Rakhlin, Shamir & Sridharan, 2012, Theorem 1) applies. With a decaying step size $\eta_t = \eta_0/(1+\alpha t)$ and $\eta_0 \ge 1/(\alpha\lambda)$, we obtain
\mathbb{E}\bigl[\|\hat\theta_t - \theta^\ast\|_2^2\bigr] \le \frac{C}{1+\alpha t}, \qquad C = \max\Bigl(\|\hat\theta_0-\theta^\ast\|_2^2,\; \frac{4G^2\eta_0^2}{\alpha\lambda\eta_0-1}\Bigr).


Thus the mean‑squared error decreases as $\mathcal{O}(1/t)$.

Remark. Without the proximal term ($\lambda=0$), the function is only convex (not strongly), and the best achievable rate would be $\mathcal{O}(1/\sqrt{t})$. The population prior thus not only accelerates personalisation but also guarantees a tighter convergence bound. $\square$

