A.2 Theorem 2.2 (Trust Budget Stability) – Extended Analysis

Complete Foster‑Lyapunov argument.
Proof (expanded). For the budget process $B_t$, define $V(B) = (B - B^\ast)^2$. Let $\Delta B = B_{t+1} - B_t$. The expected drift is
\mathbb{E}[V(B_{t+1}) - V(B_t) \mid B_t = B] = 2(B - B^\ast)\,\delta(B) + \mathbb{E}[(\Delta B)^2 \mid B_t = B],


where $\delta(B) = \beta p_a(B) - \alpha p_d(B) - \mu p_i(B)$. Since $p_a,p_d,p_i$ are (by stationarity) independent of $B$ inside the interior of the interval, $\delta$ is constant. Also $\mathbb{E}[(\Delta B)^2] \le \alpha^2$ (worst‑case step is $\pm\alpha$). Therefore,
\mathbb{E}[V(B_{t+1}) - V(B_t) \mid B_t = B] \le 2(B - B^\ast)\delta + \alpha^2.

When $\delta < 0$, the term $2(B - B^\ast)\delta$ is negative for $B > B^\ast$ and positive for $B < B^\ast$. Choosing $B^\ast$ such that $-\delta \gg 0$, the drift is strongly negative for $|B - B^\ast| > \alpha^2/(2|\delta|)$. The Foster‑Lyapunov criterion guarantees positive recurrence. The stationary distribution $\pi$ satisfies $\mathbb{E}_\pi[B] = B^\ast$ and $\operatorname{Var}_\pi(B) = \mathcal{O}(\alpha^2/\delta^2)$. The false‑positive rate $\mathrm{FPR}(B)$ is monotonically decreasing in $B$ (higher budget → tighter thresholds), so $\limsup_{t\to\infty} \mathbb{E}[\mathrm{FPR}(B_t)] \le \mathrm{FPR}(B^\ast) + O(\alpha^2/\delta^2)$. This completes the proof. $\square$

