APPENDIX B — NEURAL ARCHITECTURE AND MODEL SPECIFICATIONS

B.1 TCN Encoder Detailed Configuration

Layer / Block Type Kernel Dilation Channels Activation Normalization
1 Causal Conv1D 3 1 64 ReLU LayerNorm
2 Causal Conv1D 3 2 64 ReLU LayerNorm
3 Causal Conv1D 3 4 64 ReLU LayerNorm
4 Causal Conv1D 3 8 64 ReLU LayerNorm

Residual connections are present after each block; the first block’s residual is a $1\times1$ convolution projecting input channels (20 = 12 continuous + 8 binary) to 64. Output heads: two separate linear layers that map the final hidden state to $\mu_{Z_t}$ and $\log\sigma^2_{Z_t}$, both of dimension 64.

Receptive field: $1 + 2 \times (1+2+4+8) = 31$ time‑steps ≡ 155 minutes at 5‑minute resolution.

B.2 GRU Dynamics Prior

· Input: $Z_t \in \mathbb{R}^{64}$ (previous latent sample)
· Hidden state size: $d_h = 64$
· Two linear layers from the hidden state:
  · $\mu_{dyn} = W_\mu h_t + b_\mu$
  · $\sigma_{dyn} = \text{softplus}(W_\sigma h_t + b_\sigma)$
· Number of parameters: $3 \times (64 \times 64 + 64) \approx 12{,}480$ for the GRU itself, plus $64\times 64$ for each emission head → about 29 000 total.

B.3 Decoder

· Single linear layer: $Z_t \mapsto \mathbb{R}^{20}$ (12 continuous means + 8 logits).
· Continuous log‑variances are learned per feature (12 scalars).
· Binary heads: logits passed through sigmoid.

B.4 Guardian MLP Classifier

Layer Input Size Output Size Activation
1 8 32 ReLU
2 32 32 ReLU
3 32 16 ReLU
4 16 5 Softmax (temperature $T_{cal}$)

Total parameters ≈ 2 500. Calibration temperature $T_{cal}$ is optimised on a held‑out set using cross‑entropy.

B.5 GP Fallback Model

For each continuous feature $i$, we maintain a Gaussian Process with Matérn‑3/2 kernel:
k_i(t,t') = \sigma_{f,i}^2 \left(1 + \frac{\sqrt{3}|t-t'|}{\ell_i}\right) \exp\!\left(-\frac{\sqrt{3}|t-t'|}{\ell_i}\right).


The hyperparameters $\sigma_{f,i}^2$ (signal variance) and $\ell_i$ (lengthscale) are fitted to the most recent 7 days of data using maximum likelihood. Binary features are handled by a simple Bernoulli rate estimator with beta‑prior smoothing.

The composite anomaly score is a weighted sum of standardised residuals:
\Delta_t^{GP} = \sum_{i=1}^{d_c} \lambda_i \frac{|x^{(i)}_t - \hat{x}^{(i)}_t|}{\hat{\sigma}_i},


where $\hat{\sigma}_i$ is the predictive standard deviation and $\lambda_i$ are importance weights derived from a global sensitivity analysis. This lightweight fallback consumes less than 3 % of the battery budget.

