APPENDIX C — INTERVENTION POLICY AND COST FUNCTION DETAILS

C.1 Sleep Probability Model

A two‑state Hidden Markov Model (sleep/wake) is trained per user on HRV, actigraphy, and time‑of‑day features. The emission distributions are:

· Wake: $\mathcal{N}(\mu_{wake}, \Sigma_{wake})$ over a 3‑dimensional vector (HRV mean, step variance, log‑light).
· Sleep: $\mathcal{N}(\mu_{sleep}, \Sigma_{sleep})$ with lower variance.

Transition probabilities are time‑dependent via a sinusoidal function of hour. The forward algorithm gives $P_{sleep}(t) = P(S_t = \text{sleep} \mid \text{observations})$. A hard block is raised when $P_{sleep}(t) > 0.8$.

C.2 Receptivity Model

A logistic regression model is maintained per user:
\operatorname{logit} P(\text{accept} \mid t, B_t, \hat{c}_t) = \gamma_0 + \gamma_1 \cdot \text{hour\_sin}(t) + \gamma_2 \cdot \text{hour\_cos}(t) + \gamma_3 \cdot B_t + \sum_{c} \eta_c \mathbb{I}[\hat{c}_t = c].


The weights are updated online via a single stochastic gradient step after each intervention outcome.

C.3 Cost Function Calibration

The weights $w_1,\dots,w_5$ in the cost function $\mathcal{C}(t)$ are learned via inverse reinforcement learning from logged human‑over‑ride patterns. Starting from the defaults $(2.0, 5.0, 1.0, 0.5, 3.0)$, a Bayesian optimisation loop adjusts them every 30 days to minimise the number of user‑dismissed notifications while maintaining tier‑appropriate escalation rates.

